WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.708 --> 00:00:05.166
SPEAKER 1: We begin
with a problem.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.740
Alice and Bob live
in tree forts which

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:21.060
are far apart, with no
line of sight between them.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.090
And they need to communicate.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:26.285
So they decide to run a
wire between the two houses.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:52.270
They pull the wire tight and
attach a tin can to each end,

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:55.350
allowing them to send their
voices faintly along the wire.

00:01:01.554 --> 00:01:03.048
SPEAKER 2: Can you hear me?

00:01:03.048 --> 00:01:05.040
[INAUDIBLE]

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:09.104
SPEAKER 3: I can hear
you, but just barely.

00:01:09.104 --> 00:01:10.020
SPEAKER 2: [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:15.815
SPEAKER 1: However,
there is a problem.

00:01:18.595 --> 00:01:20.200
Noise.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:22.640
Whenever there is
a high wind, it

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.135
becomes impossible to hear
the signal over the noise.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:32.310
So they need a way to increase
the energy level of the signal

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:35.020
to separate it from the noise.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:36.245
This gives Bob an idea.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:42.960
They can simply
pluck the wire, which

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.446
is much easier to
detect over the noise.

00:01:46.446 --> 00:01:49.050
But this leads to a new problem.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:52.420
How do they encode their
messages as plucks?

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:00.100
Well, since they want to play
board games across a distance,

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.276
they tackle the most
common messages first.

00:02:03.276 --> 00:02:06.010
The outcome of two dice rolls.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:08.610
In this case, the
messages they are sending

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:12.520
can be thought of as a
selection from a finite number

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:13.740
of symbols.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.300
In this case, the 11
possible numbers, which we

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.710
call a discrete source.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:27.200
At first, they decide to
use the simplest method.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.840
They send the result as
the number of plucks.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.940
So to send a three,
they send three plucks.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.405
Nine is nine plucks.

00:02:35.405 --> 00:02:38.230
And 12 is 12 plucks.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.240
However, they soon
realize that this

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.420
takes much longer
than it needs to.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.470
From practice, they find that
their maximum pluck speed

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:51.000
is two plucks per second.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.700
Any faster and they
will get confused.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.180
So two plucks per
second can be thought

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.670
of as the rate, or capacity
for sending information

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:00.270
in this way.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.440
And it turns out that
the most common roll

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:12.450
is a seven, so it
takes 3.5 seconds

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:13.950
to send the number seven.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:21.870
[WIRE VIBRATIONS]

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.570
Alice then realizes
they can do much better

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.330
if they change their
coding strategy.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:30.090
She realizes that the odds
of each number being sent

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:31.760
follows a simple pattern.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.910
There is one way to roll
a two, two ways to rule

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:38.585
is three, three ways
to roll a four, four

00:03:38.585 --> 00:03:42.866
ways to roll a five,
five ways to roll a six,

00:03:42.866 --> 00:03:46.340
and six ways to rule a
seven, the most common.

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:50.140
And five ways to roll an
eight, four ways for a nine,

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:53.960
and so on, back to
one way for a 12.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:57.100
And this is a graph showing the
number of ways each result can

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.290
occur, and the
pattern is obvious.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:02.910
So now let's change
the graph to number

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:05.380
of plucks versus each symbol.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.120
She proceeds by mapping the
most common number, seven,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.585
to the shortest
signal, one pluck.

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.160
She then proceeds to the
next most probable number,

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.910
and if there is a tie,
she picks one at random.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:22.970
In this case, she selects
six to be two plucks,

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:26.940
and then eight to be three
plucks, and then back to five

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:30.290
to be four plucks, and
nine as five plucks,

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:33.060
and back and forth
until we reach

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:36.470
12, which is assigned
to 11 plucks.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:39.470
Now the most common
number, seven,

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.970
can be sent in less than a
second, a huge improvement.

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:47.750
This symbol change allows
them to send more information

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:52.170
in the same amount
of time on average.

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:56.080
In fact, this coding strategy is
optimal for this simple example

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.370
in that it's impossible for
you to come up with a shorter

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:05.040
method of sending two dice
rolls using identical plucks.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.960
However, after playing with
the wire for some time,

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.310
Bob hits on a new idea.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:56.146
[WIRE VIBRATIONS]

